00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.840 align:start position:0%
don't be fooled by the big mouth basking  sharks sluggish demeanor while it spends  most of its time slowly slurping its way  through plankton filled waters every  once in a while this mammoth fish the  second largest in the sea hurls its body  entirely out of the water a team of  international scientists use video  analysis and a data logger to measure  the vertical velocity of basking shark  breaches off the coast of Ireland in  this video the researchers have equipped  a male basking shark with an accelerator  in just 10 beats of its tail or nine  seconds the fish propels itself from a  depth of 28 meters to the water surface  a miracle in the study they clocked some  of the animal speeds at up to five  meters per second just a scooch faster  than their great white shark cousins and  these jumps are energetically costly for  the plankton eaters scientists estimate  that a single breach cost the basking  shark 50% more of their daily caloric  intake than great whites of a similar  size it's thought that great white  sharks breach to snatch meals lingering  near the ocean surface why filtered  feeding sharks exhibit this behavior is  not known basking sharks might be trying  to rid themselves of parasites ward off  predators or perhaps court mates in  nearby waters but regardless of the  reason researchers believe these new  data should shift our perspective on  other supposedly slow-going creatures  [Music]
 

